etymology:
Smoczyński describes the etymology as unknown, but presents the possibilities:
# From o-grade stem of Proto-Indo-European *gʷelbʰ- (“womb”) (whence Ancient Greek δελφύς (delphús) and Sanskrit गर्भ (garbha)), provided the acute developed later on;
# Related to galėti, from Proto-Indo-European *gelH- (“able”);
# Standing in connection to synonymous šelpti, pointing at a possible phonetic alternation š- / k- with additional voicing.

Inherited from Old Lithuanian patógus (“righteous, just”), with further etymology disputed:
# potentially from Proto-Indo-European *teh₂g- (“to order, arrange, establish”);
# derived from tógti, from Proto-Indo-European *teh₂g- (“to touch, grasp”);
# or from the same root as tekėti.
